export const T6={title:'Range of motion and passive ROM (Skill V)',hook:'After a stroke, a spinal cord injury or a long ICU stay, joints that are not moved begin to stiffen within days. Muscles shorten, capsules tighten and contractures can make dressing or walking impossible. Passive range of motion keeps joints moving when patients cannot do it themselves. Skill V is the highest-point skill—82 points in 10 minutes.',objectives:['Use movement terms such as flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, rotation and circumduction correctly.','Explain why PROM is used and its precautions.','Perform PROM of the upper and lower extremity on one side following HOSA Skill V.','Use proper draping, hand placement and body mechanics during PROM.'],sections:[
['Movement vocabulary',[
'Movements are described from **anatomical position**. **Flexion** decreases the angle between bones (bending the elbow); **extension** increases it (straightening). **Abduction** moves a part **away from the midline**; **adduction** moves it **toward** the midline. **Medial (internal) rotation** turns a part toward the midline; **lateral (external) rotation** turns it away. **Circumduction** combines movements in a circle. At the forearm, **supination** turns the palm **up** and **pronation** turns it **down**.',
'Special terms apply to the wrist, ankle and hand. **Radial deviation** moves the hand toward the thumb side and **ulnar deviation** toward the little-finger side. **Dorsiflexion** pulls the toes and foot **up** toward the knee; **plantar flexion** points them **down**. **Inversion** turns the sole **inward** and **eversion** **outward**. **Opposition** touches the thumb to each fingertip.',
match('Match the movement',[['Abduction','Away from the midline'],['Adduction','Toward the midline'],['Supination','Palm up'],['Pronation','Palm down'],['Dorsiflexion','Toes and foot up toward the knee'],['Plantar flexion','Toes and foot pointed down'],['Inversion','Sole turned inward'],['Ulnar deviation','Hand toward the little-finger side']])]],
['Why PROM and when to stop',[
'**PROM** moves a joint through its available range with **no muscle effort** from the patient. It maintains joint and soft-tissue mobility, reduces the risk of **contractures**, helps circulation and sensory awareness and prepares for active movement. It does **not** build strength. Normal adult ranges are measured with a **goniometer** (for example, shoulder flexion about **0–180°**, elbow flexion about **0–150°**, knee flexion about **0–135°**, hip flexion about **0–120°**, ankle dorsiflexion about **0–20°**).',
'Move each joint **slowly and smoothly**, within the **pain-free range**, supporting the limb **above and below the joint**. **Stop and report** to the PT if the patient complains of **pain**, shows **profuse perspiration** or has **difficulty breathing**. PROM is used cautiously or avoided after fresh fractures, over unstable joints, with suspected **deep vein thrombosis** or when a surgeon restricts motion.',
sort('Continue or stop and report?',['Continue gently','Stop and report to the PT'],[['Patient says the stretch feels tight but not painful',0],['Patient grimaces and reports sharp pain',1],['Patient begins sweating heavily',1],['Patient breathing becomes labored',1],['Joint moves smoothly through range',0]])]],
['Setting up: Skill V steps 1–12',[
'**1.** Obtain instructions from the PT, including the number of **repetitions (3–5)** and any joint-specific instructions. **2.** Knock or request permission, close the door or screen the unit. **3.** Greet and introduce yourself. **4.** Identify the patient. **5.** Hand hygiene. **6.** Explain and obtain consent. **7.** Lock the wheels of the bed or mat table. **8.** Raise the bed to a comfortable **working height**. **9.** Lower the side rail on your working side.',
'**10.** Position the patient **supine** in good body alignment. **11.** **Drape** the patient with bed linen or a bath blanket. **12.** Exercise the **correct side** as stated in the scenario. Some steps (side rails, bed height) apply only in a hospital bed and may be adjusted for an outpatient mat table. Throughout, keep consistent **draping** for modesty while still seeing the limb, check in with the patient about comfort and use proper **body mechanics**.',
order('Before the first joint',['Obtain instructions and repetitions','Knock, privacy, greet, identify','Hand hygiene; explain and obtain consent','Lock wheels; raise the bed; lower the near rail','Position supine in good alignment','Drape the patient','Confirm the correct side'])]],
['Upper extremity: Skill V steps 13–16',[
'**Shoulder (13):** for abduction/adduction, grasp the **wrist and elbow**, move the arm **away from the trunk** and back to the side. For flexion/extension, the shoulder is at the edge of the surface; for the left shoulder, grasp the **left wrist with your left hand and the elbow with your right** (alternate for the right side); raise the arm **above the head** and return it to the side. **Elbow (14):** support the elbow and wrist; **flex** by bending the forearm toward the shoulder and **extend** by straightening; **pronate** (palm down) and **supinate** (palm up).',
'**Wrist (15):** grasp the hand over the palmar and dorsal surfaces while stabilizing the forearm; **flex** (hand down toward the forearm), **extend** (straighten), and deviate in the **ulnar** (toward the little finger) and **radial** (toward the thumb) directions. **Fingers and thumb (16):** support the wrist; **flex** toward the palm and **extend**; **abduct** (spread apart) and **adduct** (bring together); perform **opposition** (thumb to each fingertip) and **circumduct** the thumb in a circle.',
order('Upper extremity sequence',['Shoulder abduction and adduction','Shoulder flexion and extension','Elbow flexion and extension','Forearm pronation and supination','Wrist flexion, extension, ulnar and radial deviation','Finger and thumb flexion, extension, abduction, adduction','Thumb opposition and circumduction'])]],
['Lower extremity: Skill V steps 17–29',[
'**Hip (17):** support the leg with one hand **under the knee** and the other **under the ankle**; **abduct** the whole leg out to the side and **adduct** it back; **flex** by bending the knee and moving the thigh toward the abdomen and **extend** by straightening; for **medial and lateral rotation**, grasp the distal thigh and the area above the ankle and **roll the limb inward and outward**. **Knee (18):** with the same support, **flex** by bending the lower leg toward the thigh and **extend** by straightening.',
'**Ankle (19):** one hand under the foot and the other behind the ankle; **dorsiflex** (toes up toward the knee), **plantar flex** (toes down), **invert** (turn inward) and **evert** (turn outward). **Toes (20):** rest the leg on the bed; **abduct**, **adduct**, **flex** and **extend**. **21–23.** Keep draping consistent, check comfort and use good body mechanics. **24.** Straighten the linens and remove the bath blanket. **25.** Raise the side rail. **26.** Hand hygiene. **27.** Communicate. **28.** Verbalize that you would **stop and report** if the patient had pain, profuse perspiration or difficulty breathing. **29.** Report treatment and observations to the PT.',
flip('Hand placement cues',[['Shoulder','Wrist and elbow'],['Elbow','One hand at the elbow, one at the wrist'],['Wrist','Grasp the hand; stabilize the forearm'],['Hip and knee','Under the knee and under the ankle'],['Hip rotation','Distal thigh and above the ankle; roll in and out'],['Ankle','Under the foot and behind the ankle']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('PROM decisions',{a:{text:'The PT asks for PROM of the right side, 3 repetitions each. You begin shoulder flexion and the patient says, “Ouch—that’s sharp.” What do you do?',choices:[['Stop the motion, move only in the pain-free range and report to the PT','b','Correct. PROM stays within the pain-free range.',true],['Push a little further to stretch it','b','Forcing through pain can cause injury.',false]]},b:{text:'During hip rotation, the blanket slips and exposes the patient. What now?',choices:[['Readjust the drape to protect modesty while keeping the limb visible','c','Correct. Consistent draping earns points and respects dignity.',true],['Ignore it until the end','c','Draping should be maintained throughout.',false]]},c:{text:'You finish the toes, straighten linens, raise the rail, wash your hands and report to the PT.',end:true}}),
label('joint','Structures that stiffen without movement',[['capsule','Joint capsule','Tightens when the joint is not moved.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Produces fluid that nourishes cartilage with movement.'],['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Needs motion and loading for nutrition.'],['ligament','Ligament','Shortens with immobility.']])]]
],takeaways:['Movement terms: flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, rotation, circumduction, supination/pronation, radial/ulnar deviation, dorsiflexion/plantar flexion, inversion/eversion, opposition.','PROM keeps joints mobile without strengthening; move slowly within the pain-free range, support above and below the joint, and stop and report for pain, profuse sweating or breathing difficulty.','Skill V: instructions (3–5 reps), privacy, identify, lock and raise the bed, supine, drape, correct side; shoulder, elbow, forearm, wrist, fingers, thumb, hip, knee, ankle, toes; maintain draping, check comfort, report.'],terms:[['Passive range of motion','Movement of a joint by another person with no effort from the patient.'],['Contracture','Permanent shortening of tissues that limits joint motion.'],['Goniometer','A tool for measuring joint angles.']],mcqs:[q('Moving the arm away from the body is:','Abduction','Adduction','Flexion','Pronation','Away from the midline.'),q('Turning the palm up is:','Supination','Pronation','Inversion','Eversion','Like holding soup.'),q('Pulling the toes up toward the knee is:','Dorsiflexion','Plantar flexion','Eversion','Abduction','Plantar flexion points down.'),q('How many repetitions are typical in Skill V?','3–5','20–30','1','50','Per PT instructions.'),q('Where are your hands during hip and knee PROM?','Under the knee and under the ankle','On the foot only','On the thigh only','On the patient’s abdomen','Support above and below the joint.'),q('What should you do if the patient complains of pain during PROM?','Stop and report to the physical therapist','Push through it','Speed up','Ignore it','Stay within the pain-free range.'),q('Touching the thumb to each fingertip is:','Opposition','Circumduction','Abduction','Deviation','Used in grasping.'),q('Does PROM build muscle strength?','No','Yes, a lot','Only in the legs','Only if done quickly','The patient does not contract muscles.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to perform passive range of motion of the lower extremity on one side following HOSA Skill V, including setup, hand placement, each motion and safety checks.',rubric:[['Setup',['instructions','repetitions','privacy','identify','lock','height','supine','drape','correct side']],['Hip motions',['under the knee','under the ankle','abduct','adduct','flex','extend','rotation','roll']],['Knee, ankle and toes',['knee','dorsiflex','plantar','invert','evert','toes','abduct','flex']],['Safety and finish',['pain','perspiration','breathing','stop','report','comfort','body mechanics','side rail']]],model:'I obtain instructions from the PT, including 3–5 repetitions and which side to exercise, knock and provide privacy, greet and identify the patient, perform hand hygiene and obtain consent. I lock the bed wheels, raise the bed to a comfortable working height, lower the near side rail, position the patient supine in good alignment and drape them with a bath blanket so only the limb is visible. For the hip, I support the leg with one hand under the knee and the other under the ankle, abduct the whole leg out to the side and adduct it back, flex the hip by bending the knee and bringing the thigh toward the abdomen and extend it by straightening the leg, then grasp the distal thigh and the area above the ankle to roll the leg inward and outward for medial and lateral rotation. For the knee, I flex by bending the lower leg toward the thigh and extend by straightening. For the ankle, with one hand under the foot and one behind the ankle, I dorsiflex, plantar flex, invert and evert. For the toes, with the leg resting on the bed, I abduct, adduct, flex and extend them. Each motion is slow and smooth within the pain-free range. I keep the drape in place, check comfort and use good body mechanics, and I would stop and report to the PT if the patient had pain, profuse perspiration or difficulty breathing. Finally I straighten the linens, remove the bath blanket, raise the rail, perform hand hygiene and report to the PT.'},sources:[RULES,APTA_MOVES,OSA_MOVE,OSA_SYN]};

export const T7={title:'Integumentary conditions, modalities and cold application (Skill VI)',hook:'A pressure injury on the sacrum of a bedbound patient, a venous ulcer above the ankle that will not heal, a burn scar that pulls a child’s elbow into a bent position—physical therapists treat all of these. They also use modalities such as ice, heat and electrical stimulation to manage pain and swelling. Integumentary conditions are 14% of the test, and Skill VI tests cold application.',objectives:['Classify pressure injuries by stage and compare venous, arterial and neuropathic wounds.','Describe burn depth, the rule of nines and PT’s role in burn care.','Explain the effects, indications and contraindications of cold and heat and other modalities.','Apply an ice bag or cold pack following HOSA Skill VI.'],sections:[
['Pressure injuries',[
'**Pressure injuries** develop where skin over a bony area is compressed—the **sacrum, heels, hips (greater trochanters), ischial tuberosities, elbows and back of the head**. Shear, friction, moisture, poor nutrition and immobility increase risk; the **Braden Scale** scores that risk. The National Pressure Injury Advisory Panel stages them: **Stage 1**—intact skin with **non-blanchable redness**; **Stage 2**—partial-thickness loss with exposed **dermis**; **Stage 3**—full-thickness skin loss with visible **fat**; **Stage 4**—full-thickness loss with exposed **fascia, muscle, tendon, ligament, cartilage or bone**.',
'**Unstageable** injuries are obscured by **slough or eschar**, and a **deep tissue pressure injury** shows persistent deep red, maroon or purple discoloration. Prevention is the priority: reposition at least every **2 hours** in bed, use pressure-redistributing mattresses and cushions, keep skin clean and dry, avoid dragging patients (shear) and teach wheelchair users to perform **pressure relief** (weight shifts) regularly.',
match('Pressure injury stages',[['Stage 1','Intact skin, non-blanchable redness'],['Stage 2','Partial-thickness, exposed dermis'],['Stage 3','Full-thickness, fat visible'],['Stage 4','Exposed bone, tendon or muscle'],['Unstageable','Covered by slough or eschar'],['Deep tissue injury','Persistent maroon or purple skin']])]],
['Vascular and neuropathic wounds',[
'**Venous ulcers** result from **venous insufficiency**: faulty valves let blood pool in the legs. They appear on the **lower leg**, especially above the **medial malleolus**, are **shallow and irregular** with a lot of drainage and come with swelling and brownish skin staining. Treatment centers on **compression** and elevation. **Arterial ulcers** come from poor blood supply; they appear on the **toes, feet or lateral malleolus**, look **punched out**, pale and dry, are very painful and come with weak pulses and hairless, shiny skin—**compression is contraindicated** until blood flow is evaluated.',
'**Neuropathic (diabetic) ulcers** form on the **plantar surface** of the foot, often under the metatarsal heads, in people with loss of sensation; they may not hurt. Treatment includes **offloading** pressure (special boots or casts), blood sugar control and daily foot checks. PTs contribute with wound **debridement** (removing dead tissue) where allowed, dressings, compression, electrical stimulation, offloading, exercise and education.',
sort('Venous, arterial or neuropathic?',['Venous','Arterial','Neuropathic'],[['Above the medial malleolus with heavy drainage',0],['Punched-out wound on a toe, very painful',1],['Under the metatarsal heads in a patient with diabetes',2],['Treated with compression and elevation',0],['Weak pulses and shiny, hairless skin',1],['Painless because sensation is lost',2]]),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Sample question',text:'HOSA asks where wounds caused by venous insufficiency are most commonly found. The answer is the **lower part of the leg**.'}]],
['Burns',[
'Burns are classified by depth. **Superficial** burns involve the **epidermis** (red, painful, no blisters—like sunburn). **Superficial partial-thickness** burns extend into the upper dermis with **blisters** and severe pain. **Deep partial-thickness** burns reach the deeper dermis, look mottled and heal with scarring. **Full-thickness** burns destroy the epidermis and dermis, look white, brown or leathery and are **painless** because nerve endings are destroyed; they usually need grafting.',
'The adult **rule of nines** estimates total body surface area: head and neck **9%**, each arm **9%**, front of the trunk **18%**, back of the trunk **18%**, each leg **18%** and perineum **1%**. PTs position patients to prevent **contractures** (the position of comfort is often the position of contracture), splint, provide ROM and exercise, use **pressure garments** to manage hypertrophic scarring and help patients regain function.',
{t:'w',kind:'burns',title:'Estimate burn size with the rule of nines',prompt:'Select burned regions to calculate total body surface area.'},
label('skin','Skin layers and burn depth',[['epidermis','Epidermis','Superficial burns stay here.'],['dermis','Dermis','Partial-thickness burns extend into it.'],['hypodermis','Subcutaneous layer','Full-thickness burns can reach here.'],['burn1','Superficial burn','Red and painful.'],['burn2','Partial-thickness burn','Blisters.'],['burn3','Full-thickness burn','Leathery and painless.']])]],
['Cold, heat and other modalities',[
'**Cryotherapy** (cold) causes **vasoconstriction**, reduces swelling, slows nerve conduction (reducing pain) and decreases muscle spasm. It is used after acute injury and after exercise. Typical applications last **15–20 minutes**. Contraindications and precautions include **Raynaud’s phenomenon**, **cold urticaria** (hives from cold), **cryoglobulinemia**, **impaired sensation**, poor circulation, open wounds and patients who cannot report discomfort. Never apply ice directly to skin for long periods.',
'**Thermotherapy** (heat)—hot packs, paraffin, warm whirlpool—causes **vasodilation**, increases tissue extensibility and relaxes muscles; it is used for **chronic** stiffness, not acute swelling, and is contraindicated over **acute inflammation**, bleeding, malignancy or areas of impaired sensation. **Therapeutic ultrasound** heats deep tissues. **Electrical stimulation** includes **TENS** for pain and **NMES** to strengthen muscles; it is contraindicated over a **pacemaker**, the pregnant uterus or the carotid sinus. **Traction** and **compression** devices are other modalities.',
sort('Cold or heat?',['Cold (cryotherapy)','Heat (thermotherapy)'],[['Ankle sprain one hour ago',0],['Chronic neck stiffness before stretching',1],['Swelling after knee surgery',0],['Paraffin for arthritic hands',1],['Causes vasoconstriction',0],['Increases tissue extensibility',1]])]],
['Cold application: Skill VI',[
'Skill VI has 19 steps in 6 minutes. **1.** Obtain instructions from the PT. **2.** Assemble equipment. **3.** Greet and introduce yourself. **4.** Identify the patient. **5.** Hand hygiene. **6.** Explain and obtain consent. **7.** Apply gloves if the area has **drainage**. **8.** Provide privacy. The scenario uses **either an ice bag or a disposable pack**. **Ice bag (9):** **fill it with water to check for leaks**, then empty it; use a **scoop** to fill it **half full** of ice; place it on a flat surface to **expel all air** and seal it; **wipe the outside dry**. **Disposable pack (10):** **activate** the chemicals by squeezing or striking it against a solid surface.',
'**11.** Place a **cover** on the pack or wrap it in a **towel** and tape the towel. **12.** Place it on the **correct area** in the scenario. **13.** Make sure the patient is comfortable and the pack is positioned correctly. **14.** Verbalize that you would **recheck every 5–7 minutes** for unusual changes in skin color compared with untreated areas or complaints of poor tolerance. **15.** When the judge says the time is up, remove it and **note the skin condition and patient reaction**. **16.** Clean or replace equipment; discard disposables; linens to the hamper. **17.** Hand hygiene. **18.** Communicate. **19.** Report to the PT.',
order('Ice bag preparation',['Fill with water to check for leaks; empty','Scoop ice to half full','Lay flat to expel air; seal','Wipe the outside dry','Cover or wrap in a towel and tape','Place on the correct area; recheck every 5–7 minutes']),
chk('How often should skin be rechecked during cold application in Skill VI?',['Every 5–7 minutes','Only at the end','Every 30 minutes','Never'],'Look for unusual color changes or poor tolerance.')]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Modality safety',{a:{text:'The PT orders an ice bag for a patient’s acute ankle sprain. The patient mentions that her fingers turn white and numb in the cold. What should you do?',choices:[['Report it to the PT before applying cold—this may be Raynaud’s phenomenon','b','Correct. Cold sensitivity is a contraindication or precaution.',true],['Apply the ice for extra time','b','Cold could worsen her symptoms.',false]]},b:{text:'The PT approves a short application over a towel. At the 5-minute check, the skin looks very pale compared with the other ankle. What now?',choices:[['Remove the ice, note the skin condition and report to the PT','c','Correct. Unusual color change means poor tolerance.',true],['Leave it on—that is normal','c','Marked pallor compared with untreated skin warrants removal.',false]]},c:{text:'You document and report the reaction. Skin checks protect patients.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Pressure injuries: Stage 1 non-blanchable redness to Stage 4 exposed bone, plus unstageable and deep tissue; reposition every 2 hours and relieve pressure.','Venous ulcers: lower leg above the medial malleolus, compression; arterial: toes and lateral malleolus, punched out, no compression; neuropathic: plantar foot, offloading; burns by depth and rule of nines.','Cold causes vasoconstriction for acute injury (15–20 minutes, avoid with Raynaud’s or poor sensation); Skill VI: check the bag for leaks, half full, expel air, dry, cover, place, recheck every 5–7 minutes, note skin and report.'],terms:[['Venous insufficiency','Poor return of blood through the leg veins, causing pooling and swelling.'],['Cryotherapy','Therapeutic use of cold.'],['Debridement','Removal of dead tissue from a wound.']],mcqs:[q('Where are wounds caused by venous insufficiency most commonly found?','Lower part of the leg','Sacrum','Elbow','Iliac crest','HOSA sample question.'),q('A Stage 1 pressure injury is:','Intact skin with non-blanchable redness','An open wound with exposed bone','Covered by eschar','A blister only on the hands','Earliest stage.'),q('Which ulcer is treated with compression?','Venous','Arterial','Neuropathic','Burn','Arterial ulcers need blood flow evaluated first.'),q('A full-thickness burn is often:','Painless and leathery','Red and blanching','Mildly painful with no blisters','Only in the epidermis','Nerve endings are destroyed.'),q('In the adult rule of nines, each leg is:','18%','9%','1%','36%','Front and back combined.'),q('How full should the ice bag be in Skill VI?','Half full','Completely full','One-quarter full','Empty','Then expel air.'),q('Which is a contraindication to cold?','Raynaud’s phenomenon','Acute ankle sprain','Swelling after surgery','Muscle spasm','Cold sensitivity disorder.'),q('Electrical stimulation is contraindicated over:','A cardiac pacemaker','The quadriceps','The lower back','The shoulder','Can interfere with the device.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare venous and arterial ulcers, and explain how you would apply an ice bag following HOSA Skill VI, including safety checks and contraindications.',rubric:[['Venous ulcers',['venous','lower leg','medial malleolus','drainage','shallow','compression']],['Arterial ulcers',['arterial','toes','lateral malleolus','punched','painful','no compression']],['Ice bag technique',['leaks','scoop','half','expel air','dry','towel','cover','correct area']],['Safety',['5-7 minutes','skin','color','tolerance','raynaud','sensation','report']]],model:'Venous ulcers are caused by venous insufficiency, which lets blood pool in the legs. They appear on the lower leg, especially above the medial malleolus, are shallow and irregular with heavy drainage, and come with swelling; compression and elevation are key treatments. Arterial ulcers are caused by poor arterial blood supply. They appear on the toes, feet or lateral malleolus, look punched out, pale and dry, are very painful and come with weak pulses, so compression is avoided until blood flow is evaluated. To apply an ice bag, I obtain the PT’s instructions, assemble equipment, greet and identify the patient, perform hand hygiene, explain the skill and obtain consent, put on gloves if there is drainage and provide privacy. I fill the bag with water to check for leaks, empty it, scoop ice to fill it half full, lay it flat to expel the air, seal it and wipe it dry, then cover it or wrap it in a towel and tape the towel. I place it on the area named in the scenario, make sure the patient is comfortable and recheck every 5–7 minutes for unusual color changes compared with untreated skin or complaints of poor tolerance. Before applying cold, I would report contraindications such as Raynaud’s phenomenon, cold urticaria or impaired sensation to the PT. When the time is up, I remove the bag, note the skin and the patient’s reaction, clean up, perform hand hygiene and report to the PT.'},sources:[RULES,NPIAP,MEDLINE_VENOUS,MEDLINE_BURNS]};

export const T8={title:'Neuromuscular conditions',hook:'Every 40 seconds someone in the United States has a stroke. Spinal cord injuries, Parkinson’s disease, multiple sclerosis and traumatic brain injuries also change how people move. Physical therapists use the brain’s ability to rewire itself—neuroplasticity—to help patients regain walking, balance and independence. Neuromuscular conditions are 16% of the test.',objectives:['Describe how stroke location affects movement, speech and perception.','Explain spinal cord injury levels, complete versus incomplete injuries and autonomic dysreflexia.','Recognize the movement features of Parkinson’s disease, MS, ALS and Guillain-Barré syndrome.','Describe PT interventions that use neuroplasticity, balance training and task-specific practice.'],sections:[
['Stroke',[
'A **stroke (cerebrovascular accident, CVA)** occurs when blood flow to the brain is blocked (**ischemic**, about 87%) or a vessel bleeds (**hemorrhagic**). Warning signs follow **BE FAST**: **B**alance loss, **E**yes (vision changes), **F**ace drooping, **A**rm weakness, **S**peech difficulty, **T**ime to call 911. Because each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body, a **left-hemisphere** stroke usually causes **right hemiplegia** (paralysis) or hemiparesis (weakness) and often **aphasia** (language problems); a **right-hemisphere** stroke causes **left** weakness and may cause **left neglect** (ignoring the left side), poor judgment and impulsivity.',
'After a stroke, muscles may first be flaccid, then develop **spasticity** (velocity-dependent resistance to stretch) and abnormal movement patterns. PT focuses on **early mobility**, task-specific training (practicing real activities such as sit-to-stand and walking), **balance**, gait training with devices or orthoses (such as an **ankle-foot orthosis** for foot drop), **constraint-induced movement therapy** (restraining the stronger arm to force use of the weaker one) and preventing complications such as shoulder subluxation and falls.',
label('brain-lateral','Brain regions and stroke effects',[['frontal','Frontal lobe','Movement planning, judgment and personality.'],['motor','Motor cortex','Controls voluntary movement on the opposite side.'],['broca','Broca’s area','Speech production; damage causes expressive aphasia.'],['wernicke','Wernicke’s area','Language understanding; damage causes receptive aphasia.'],['cerebellum','Cerebellum','Balance and coordination; damage causes ataxia.'],['occipital','Occipital lobe','Vision.']]),
sort('Left- or right-hemisphere stroke?',['Left hemisphere','Right hemisphere'],[['Right-side weakness',0],['Aphasia',0],['Left-side neglect',1],['Left-side weakness',1],['Impulsive, poor safety awareness',1],['Slow, cautious behavior',0]])]],
['Spinal cord injury',[
'**Spinal cord injuries (SCI)** are often caused by vehicle crashes, falls, violence and sports. The **neurological level** is the lowest level with normal function. **Tetraplegia** (quadriplegia) results from cervical injuries and affects the arms, trunk and legs; **paraplegia** results from thoracic, lumbar or sacral injuries and affects the trunk and legs. **Complete** injuries have no sensory or motor function below the level (including the lowest sacral segments); **incomplete** injuries preserve some function. Injuries at **C1–C4** may require a ventilator; **C5–C6** injuries preserve some shoulder and elbow function; **C7** preserves elbow extension, important for transfers.',
'**Autonomic dysreflexia** is an emergency in people with injuries at **T6 or above**: an irritant below the injury (often a **full bladder**, kinked catheter or constipation) triggers a dangerous rise in blood pressure, pounding headache, flushing and sweating above the injury. **Sit the person up**, loosen tight clothing, find and remove the cause and get help immediately. **Orthostatic hypotension**, pressure injuries, spasticity and loss of bone density are other concerns. PTs teach transfers, wheelchair skills, pressure relief, strengthening and, for some, standing and walking with orthoses.',
match('SCI terms',[['Tetraplegia','Cervical injury affecting arms, trunk and legs'],['Paraplegia','Injury below the cervical cord affecting trunk and legs'],['Complete injury','No function below the level'],['Incomplete injury','Some preserved function below the level'],['Autonomic dysreflexia','Sudden high blood pressure in T6-and-above injuries'],['C7 level','Elbow extension preserved for transfers']]),
order('Respond to autonomic dysreflexia',['Recognize pounding headache, flushing and high blood pressure','Sit the person upright','Loosen tight clothing and leg bags','Check the bladder and catheter for blockage','Check for other irritants such as constipation or tight shoes','Get medical help immediately'])]],
['Progressive neurologic diseases',[
'**Parkinson’s disease** results from loss of dopamine-producing neurons. Signs include **resting tremor**, **rigidity**, **bradykinesia** (slow movement) and **postural instability** (**TRAP**). The gait is **shuffling** and may become **festinating** (short, quickening steps) with **freezing**. PT uses large-amplitude movement training (such as LSVT BIG), cueing with rhythm or visual targets, balance and fall prevention. **Multiple sclerosis (MS)** is an autoimmune disease that destroys **myelin** in the central nervous system, causing weakness, numbness, vision problems, balance problems and **fatigue**; symptoms often worsen with **heat**, so exercise in cool settings with rest breaks.',
'**Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)** destroys **upper and lower motor neurons**, causing progressive weakness, muscle wasting and eventually breathing failure; PT focuses on maintaining function, energy conservation, equipment and comfort without overworking weakened muscles. **Guillain-Barré syndrome (GBS)** is an autoimmune attack on peripheral nerves, often after an infection, causing **ascending** weakness that may affect breathing; most patients recover over months with careful, non-fatiguing exercise. **Traumatic brain injury (TBI)** can affect movement, cognition and behavior and is scored with the Glasgow Coma Scale in the acute stage.',
"**Vestibular disorders** are another neuromuscular area. **Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo (BPPV)**, caused by loose crystals in the inner ear, produces brief spinning with head movements and is treated with **canalith repositioning** maneuvers such as the Epley maneuver. Other vestibular problems are treated with gaze-stabilization, habituation and balance exercises. **Peripheral neuropathy**, common in diabetes, reduces sensation in the feet and increases fall risk, so PTs teach foot inspection, balance training and safe footwear.",
sort('Which condition?',['Parkinson’s disease','Multiple sclerosis','ALS','Guillain-Barré syndrome'],[['Resting tremor and shuffling gait',0],['Symptoms worse with heat; fatigue',1],['Upper and lower motor neurons destroyed',2],['Ascending weakness after an infection',3],['Freezing of gait',0],['Demyelination in the central nervous system',1]])]],
['Neuroplasticity and interventions',[
'**Neuroplasticity** is the nervous system’s ability to reorganize by forming new connections. It is driven by **repetition**, **intensity**, **task specificity** (practicing the actual skill), **salience** (meaningful goals) and starting at the right time. PTs therefore design many repetitions of functional tasks—walking, reaching, standing up—rather than isolated exercises alone. **Body-weight-supported treadmill training**, **robot-assisted gait**, **functional electrical stimulation** and **virtual reality** add repetitions safely.',
'**Balance training** challenges the visual, vestibular and somatosensory systems by changing the base of support, surface and visual input. **Proprioceptive neuromuscular facilitation (PNF)** uses diagonal movement patterns. Outcome measures such as the **Berg Balance Scale**, **Timed Up and Go**, **10-meter walk test** and **6-minute walk test** track progress. PTs also prescribe **orthoses**, wheelchairs and assistive devices and educate families and caregivers.',
"Families and caregivers are essential partners. PTs teach them safe transfers, home exercise, positioning and how to recognize warning signs, because most practice happens outside therapy sessions.",
flip('Neuro intervention terms',[['Neuroplasticity','Nervous system reorganizes with practice'],['Task-specific training','Practicing the actual activity'],['Constraint-induced therapy','Restrain the strong arm to use the weak one'],['Ankle-foot orthosis','Brace for foot drop'],['PNF','Diagonal movement patterns'],['Berg Balance Scale','14-item balance test (56 points)']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Neuro emergencies',{a:{text:'During therapy, a patient with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly reports a pounding headache. His face is flushed and sweaty. What should you suspect?',choices:[['Autonomic dysreflexia','b','Correct. It is an emergency in injuries at T6 and above.',true],['Normal exercise response','b','These signs warrant immediate action.',false]]},b:{text:'What is your first action?',choices:[['Sit him upright, loosen clothing and check his catheter while calling for help','c','Correct. Lower blood pressure and remove the trigger.',true],['Lay him flat and raise his legs','c','Lying flat can raise blood pressure further.',false]]},c:{text:'The nurse finds a kinked catheter. Once it drains, his blood pressure falls. You report and document the event.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'clinical',title:'Stroke warning signs during therapy',text:'If a patient suddenly develops face drooping, arm weakness, slurred speech, vision loss or severe headache, stop the session, note the time symptoms began and activate emergency procedures. Time to treatment determines outcomes.'}]]
],takeaways:['Stroke: BE FAST; left-hemisphere strokes cause right weakness and aphasia, right-hemisphere strokes cause left weakness and neglect; PT uses early mobility, task-specific practice and orthoses.','SCI: tetraplegia (cervical) versus paraplegia; complete versus incomplete; autonomic dysreflexia (T6 and above) means sit up, loosen clothing and remove the trigger.','Parkinson’s (TRAP, shuffling and freezing), MS (demyelination, heat sensitivity), ALS (upper and lower motor neurons), GBS (ascending weakness); neuroplasticity needs repetition, intensity and task specificity.'],terms:[['Hemiplegia','Paralysis of one side of the body.'],['Spasticity','Velocity-dependent resistance to muscle stretch.'],['Neuroplasticity','The nervous system’s ability to reorganize and form new connections.']],mcqs:[q('A left-hemisphere stroke usually causes weakness on the:','Right side','Left side','Both sides','Face only','Opposite side.'),q('Left neglect is most associated with a stroke in the:','Right hemisphere','Left hemisphere','Cerebellum','Spinal cord','Ignoring the left side.'),q('Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in SCI at or above:','T6','L1','S1','C1 only','Emergency with high blood pressure.'),q('What should you do first for autonomic dysreflexia?','Sit the patient upright','Lay the patient flat','Give water','Continue exercise','Then find the trigger.'),q('A shuffling, quickening gait with freezing suggests:','Parkinson’s disease','Multiple sclerosis','Guillain-Barré syndrome','Osteoarthritis','Festinating gait.'),q('Which condition’s symptoms often worsen with heat?','Multiple sclerosis','ALS','Parkinson’s disease','Stroke','Uhthoff’s phenomenon.'),q('Guillain-Barré syndrome typically causes:','Ascending weakness','Resting tremor','Spasticity on one side','Joint cartilage loss','Often after infection.'),q('Restraining the stronger arm to force use of the weaker arm is:','Constraint-induced movement therapy','PNF','PROM','Isokinetic training','Uses neuroplasticity.')],frq:{prompt:'A 68-year-old patient had a right-hemisphere ischemic stroke. Describe the movement and perceptual problems you would expect, the safety concerns they create and three PT interventions based on neuroplasticity.',rubric:[['Expected deficits',['left','hemiparesis','hemiplegia','neglect','impulsive','spasticity']],['Safety concerns',['falls','judgment','left side','shoulder','supervision','gait belt']],['Neuroplasticity principles',['repetition','intensity','task-specific','meaningful']],['Interventions',['sit-to-stand','gait training','balance','constraint-induced','orthosis','afo','education']]],model:'Because each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body, a right-hemisphere stroke usually causes left hemiparesis or hemiplegia, sometimes with spasticity developing over time. It can also cause left neglect, in which the patient ignores the left side of the body and space, along with impulsivity and poor safety awareness, and some visual-spatial problems. These create safety concerns: the patient may try to stand or walk without help, bump into objects on the left, let the left arm hang and risk shoulder subluxation, and fall, so the PT uses a gait belt, close supervision and cues to scan to the left. Neuroplasticity depends on many repetitions, adequate intensity, task specificity and meaningful goals. Interventions include repeated task-specific practice of sit-to-stand and transfers; gait training with a cane or walker and possibly an ankle-foot orthosis for foot drop, perhaps with body-weight-supported treadmill training; balance training that varies the base of support; and constraint-induced movement therapy or intensive practice of reaching and grasping with the weaker left arm. The PT also educates the patient and family about safety and fall prevention.'},sources:[RULES,NINDS_STROKE,NINDS_SCI,NINDS_PD]};

export const T9={title:'Cardiopulmonary conditions',hook:'After a heart attack, many patients are afraid to climb stairs or walk around the block. People with COPD may feel breathless just getting dressed. Physical therapists help them exercise safely, breathe more efficiently and regain confidence—and they know exactly when to stop. Cardiopulmonary conditions are 14% of the test.',objectives:['Describe heart attack, heart failure, COPD, asthma and cystic fibrosis and their effects on exercise.','Explain the phases of cardiac rehabilitation and how exercise intensity is monitored.','Teach breathing techniques and airway clearance methods.','Recognize signs that exercise should stop.'],sections:[
['Cardiovascular conditions',[
'A **myocardial infarction (MI, heart attack)** occurs when a coronary artery is blocked and heart muscle dies. **Coronary artery disease** narrows arteries with plaque; **angina** is chest discomfort from reduced blood flow. **Heart failure** means the heart cannot pump enough blood, causing shortness of breath, fatigue and swelling in the legs; patients may gain weight from fluid. **Hypertension** strains the heart and arteries. **Peripheral artery disease** causes **intermittent claudication**—leg pain with walking that eases with rest—treated with supervised walking programs.',
'Exercise improves heart and muscle function, blood pressure, cholesterol, blood sugar, mood and quality of life. Before and during exercise, PTs monitor **heart rate**, **blood pressure**, **oxygen saturation (SpO₂)**, **respiratory rate** and symptoms. Some patients take **beta blockers**, which blunt the heart-rate response, so **perceived exertion** becomes an important guide.',
label('heart','The heart',[['lv','Left ventricle','Weakened in many types of heart failure.'],['aorta','Aorta','Carries blood to the body.'],['ra','Right atrium','Receives blood from the body.'],['rv','Right ventricle','Pumps blood to the lungs.'],['la','Left atrium','Receives blood from the lungs.']])]],
['Cardiac rehabilitation and exercise intensity',[
'**Cardiac rehabilitation** has phases. **Phase I** is **inpatient**: early mobility, education and walking in the hospital. **Phase II** is **outpatient** and supervised, often with **ECG monitoring**, typically several times a week for about 12 weeks. **Phase III** (and sometimes IV) is **maintenance**—a long-term exercise program in a community setting. Cardiac rehab reduces the risk of future heart problems and death, yet many eligible patients never attend.',
'Intensity can be estimated from heart rate. **Age-predicted maximum heart rate** is about **220 − age**. The **Karvonen (heart-rate reserve) method** sets a target: target HR = [(max HR − resting HR) × intensity] + resting HR. The **Borg Rating of Perceived Exertion (RPE)** scale runs from **6 to 20**; **11–13** (“fairly light” to “somewhat hard”) is a common moderate target. The **talk test**—being able to talk but not sing—also indicates moderate intensity.',
{t:'example',title:'Target heart rate',prompt:'A 60-year-old has a resting heart rate of 70. Find the target heart rate at 50% intensity using the Karvonen method. (Invented for practice.)',steps:['Max HR ≈ 220 − 60 = 160','Heart-rate reserve = 160 − 70 = 90','90 × 0.50 = 45','45 + 70 = 115'],answer:'Target heart rate ≈ 115 beats per minute.'},
order('Cardiac rehab phases',['Phase I: inpatient mobility and education','Phase II: supervised outpatient program, often with ECG','Phase III: long-term community maintenance'])]],
['When to stop exercise',[
'Stop exercise and notify the PT or medical team if the patient has **chest pain or pressure**, **dizziness or lightheadedness**, unusual **shortness of breath**, **confusion**, **pallor, cyanosis (blue lips) or cold sweat**, a **drop in systolic blood pressure** with increasing work (more than about 10 mm Hg), a very high blood pressure response, new **irregular heart rhythm**, **oxygen saturation** falling below the level the provider sets (often around **88–90%** for many patients), or severe leg pain.',
'Teach patients to recognize these warning signs at home, to warm up and cool down, and to avoid **holding their breath** while lifting (the **Valsalva maneuver** raises blood pressure). Patients with implanted devices or after open-heart surgery (**sternal precautions**—no heavy pushing, pulling or lifting with the arms for several weeks) need special limits.',
sort('Continue or stop?',['Continue and monitor','Stop and notify'],[['RPE of 12 and talking comfortably',0],['Chest pressure during walking',1],['Systolic BP falls as the workload increases',1],['Mild sweating and steady breathing',0],['Dizziness and pale, clammy skin',1],['SpO₂ drops below the ordered limit',1]])]],
['Pulmonary conditions',[
'**Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)**—**emphysema** and **chronic bronchitis**—limits airflow, usually from smoking. Patients have breathlessness (dyspnea), cough and fatigue and may develop a **barrel chest**. **Asthma** causes reversible airway narrowing; exercise-induced bronchospasm can be prevented with a warm-up and prescribed inhalers. **Cystic fibrosis (CF)** is a genetic disease that thickens mucus in the lungs and digestive tract. **Restrictive** diseases, such as pulmonary fibrosis and conditions after chest surgery or with neuromuscular weakness, limit lung expansion.',
'**Pulmonary rehabilitation** combines exercise, breathing training, education and energy conservation to reduce breathlessness and improve function. PTs often use the **6-minute walk test** to measure endurance and the **dyspnea scale** to rate breathlessness. Supplemental **oxygen** is a prescribed drug; follow the ordered flow rate and keep it away from flames.',
"In the **intensive care unit**, PTs help critically ill patients sit, stand and walk as early as safely possible—sometimes while they are still on a ventilator. **Early mobility** reduces **ICU-acquired weakness**, shortens hospital stays and improves function. Before mobilizing, the team checks vital signs, oxygen needs, lines and tubes, and the PT stops if the patient shows signs of intolerance.",
label('resp','The respiratory system',[['trachea','Trachea','Main airway to the lungs.'],['bronchi','Bronchi','Narrow in asthma.'],['bronchioles','Bronchioles','Obstructed in COPD.'],['alveoli','Alveoli','Destroyed in emphysema.'],['diaphragm','Diaphragm','Main breathing muscle; flattened in COPD.']])]],
['Breathing techniques and airway clearance',[
'**Pursed-lip breathing**: inhale through the nose for about 2 counts and exhale slowly through pursed lips for about 4 counts. It keeps airways open longer and reduces breathlessness, especially in COPD. **Diaphragmatic breathing** encourages the abdomen to rise with inhalation, using the diaphragm instead of neck muscles. **Incentive spirometry** helps patients take deep breaths after surgery to prevent **atelectasis** (collapsed alveoli) and pneumonia.',
'**Airway clearance** helps patients with CF, bronchiectasis or weak coughs remove mucus. Techniques include **postural drainage** (positioning so gravity drains specific lung segments), **percussion** (rhythmic clapping with cupped hands over the chest wall) and **vibration**, the **huff cough** (forced exhalation with an open throat), the **active cycle of breathing technique**, positive expiratory pressure devices and high-frequency chest wall oscillation vests. **Energy conservation** strategies—planning, pacing, sitting for tasks and pushing rather than lifting—help patients do more with less breathlessness.',
match('Technique and purpose',[['Pursed-lip breathing','Slows exhalation and reduces breathlessness'],['Diaphragmatic breathing','Uses the diaphragm instead of neck muscles'],['Incentive spirometry','Deep breaths to prevent atelectasis'],['Postural drainage','Gravity drains lung segments'],['Percussion','Clapping to loosen mucus'],['Huff cough','Forced exhalation with an open throat']])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Pulmonary rehab session',{a:{text:'A patient with COPD becomes short of breath walking down the hall. Her SpO₂ is above her ordered limit, and she has no chest pain. What do you coach?',choices:[['Pause, use pursed-lip breathing and resume when breathing settles','b','Correct. Pursed-lip breathing reduces dyspnea.',true],['Hold her breath and push through','b','Breath-holding worsens breathlessness.',false]]},b:{text:'Later, her lips look blue and her SpO₂ falls below the ordered limit. What now?',choices:[['Stop, have her sit, check her oxygen and notify the PT','c','Correct. Cyanosis and low saturation are stop signs.',true],['Increase the walking speed','c','These are warning signs.',false]]},c:{text:'The PT adjusts the plan, and you document her response.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['MI, coronary artery disease, heart failure, hypertension and PAD (claudication) all benefit from monitored exercise; beta blockers blunt heart-rate response.','Cardiac rehab Phase I inpatient, II supervised outpatient, III maintenance; max HR ≈ 220 − age, Karvonen target, Borg RPE 6–20 with 11–13 moderate; stop for chest pain, dizziness, falling BP, low SpO₂ or cyanosis.','COPD, asthma, CF and restrictive disease; pursed-lip and diaphragmatic breathing, incentive spirometry, postural drainage, percussion, huff cough and energy conservation.'],terms:[['Myocardial infarction','Death of heart muscle from a blocked coronary artery.'],['Rating of perceived exertion','A 6–20 scale of how hard exercise feels.'],['Postural drainage','Positioning that uses gravity to drain mucus from lung segments.']],mcqs:[q('Age-predicted maximum heart rate is about:','220 − age','180 − age','200 + age','100 + age','A common estimate.'),q('Which Borg RPE range is a typical moderate target?','11–13','6–7','18–20','1–3','Somewhat hard.'),q('Phase II cardiac rehab is:','Supervised outpatient exercise, often with ECG monitoring','Inpatient bed rest','Unsupervised community exercise','Surgery','After discharge.'),q('Which sign means stop exercise?','Chest pain or pressure','RPE of 12','Mild sweating','Talking comfortably','Stop and notify.'),q('Pursed-lip breathing helps by:','Keeping airways open longer during exhalation','Speeding up breathing','Holding the breath','Increasing the heart rate','Useful in COPD.'),q('Leg pain with walking that eases with rest is:','Intermittent claudication','Angina','Dyspnea','Spasticity','From peripheral artery disease.'),q('Incentive spirometry helps prevent:','Atelectasis','Spasticity','Contractures','Burns','Collapsed alveoli after surgery.'),q('Holding the breath while lifting is called:','The Valsalva maneuver','Pursed-lip breathing','Huff coughing','Diaphragmatic breathing','Raises blood pressure.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a PT would design and monitor an exercise session for a 65-year-old patient in Phase II cardiac rehabilitation who takes a beta blocker and has a resting heart rate of 65, including how to calculate intensity and when to stop.',rubric:[['Phase and setting',['phase ii','outpatient','supervised','ecg']],['Intensity calculation',['220','155','karvonen','reserve','90','target']],['Beta blocker and RPE',['beta blocker','blunt','rpe','borg','11-13','talk test']],['Monitoring and stop signs',['blood pressure','spo2','chest pain','dizziness','drop','warm-up','cool-down','valsalva']]],model:'Phase II cardiac rehabilitation is a supervised outpatient program, often with ECG monitoring, several times a week. The age-predicted maximum heart rate is about 220 − 65 = 155. Using the Karvonen method, the heart-rate reserve is 155 − 65 = 90; at 50–70% intensity the target range is about 110–128 beats per minute (90 × 0.5 + 65 = 110 and 90 × 0.7 + 65 = 128). Because this patient takes a beta blocker, which blunts the heart-rate response, heart rate may not rise as expected, so the PT also uses the Borg RPE scale, aiming for about 11–13, and the talk test. The session includes a warm-up, aerobic exercise such as treadmill walking or cycling, light resistance training with no breath-holding, since the Valsalva maneuver raises blood pressure, and a cool-down. The PT checks heart rate, blood pressure, SpO₂ and symptoms before, during and after exercise and stops and notifies the team for chest pain or pressure, dizziness, unusual shortness of breath, pallor or cyanosis, a drop in systolic blood pressure as the workload increases, a new irregular rhythm or low oxygen saturation.'},sources:[RULES,AHA_REHAB,NHLBI_COPD]};

export const T10={title:'Physical therapy for children and older adults',hook:'A baby who is not rolling over by six months, a child with cerebral palsy learning to use a walker at school, a 78-year-old who has fallen twice this year—physical therapists work across the whole lifespan. Children are not small adults, and older adults are not all frail. Pediatrics and older adults make up 16% of the test.',objectives:['Identify gross motor milestones and red flags in infant development.','Describe cerebral palsy, spina bifida, Down syndrome, muscular dystrophy and torticollis and PT’s role.','Explain early intervention and school-based PT under IDEA.','Describe normal aging, fall risk assessment and fall prevention for older adults.'],sections:[
['Motor development',[
'Gross motor skills usually develop **head to toe** (cephalocaudal) and **center outward** (proximal to distal). Typical ages vary, but many infants **hold the head steady** by about **4 months**, **roll** by about 4–6 months, **sit without support** by about **6–9 months**, **crawl** and **pull to stand** by about **9–10 months**, **walk independently** by about **12 months** (range roughly 9–18) and **run** by about 18–24 months. **Primitive reflexes** such as the Moro (startle) and asymmetric tonic neck reflex normally fade in the first months; reflexes that persist can signal a neurological problem.',
'Red flags include **not holding the head up by 4 months**, **not sitting by 9 months**, **not walking by 18 months**, loss of skills already gained, strong preference for one hand before age one, very stiff or very floppy muscle tone and persistent primitive reflexes. The CDC “Learn the Signs. Act Early.” milestones help families and providers notice delays. **Tummy time** while awake builds neck and shoulder strength and helps prevent flat spots on the head.',
order('Gross motor milestones in typical order',['Holds head steady','Rolls over','Sits without support','Crawls','Pulls to stand','Walks independently','Runs'])]],
['Common pediatric conditions',[
'**Cerebral palsy (CP)** is a group of **non-progressive** movement and posture disorders caused by injury to the developing brain before, during or soon after birth. **Spastic** CP is the most common type; others are dyskinetic and ataxic. It may affect one side (**hemiplegia**), mainly the legs (**diplegia**) or all four limbs (**quadriplegia**). **Spina bifida (myelomeningocele)** is a neural tube defect in which the spinal cord protrudes through the spine; children may have leg weakness, loss of sensation, bladder problems, **hydrocephalus** requiring a **shunt** and a high risk of **latex allergy**. Adequate **folic acid** before and during pregnancy reduces the risk.',
'**Down syndrome (trisomy 21)** brings **low muscle tone (hypotonia)**, ligament laxity and delayed milestones; some children have **atlantoaxial instability** in the neck, so certain activities are screened. **Duchenne muscular dystrophy** is an **X-linked** disease, mostly in boys, causing progressive weakness; children use **Gowers’ sign** (pushing on their thighs to stand up). **Congenital muscular torticollis** is a tight sternocleidomastoid that tilts the head; it is treated with stretching, positioning and tummy time. **Developmental dysplasia of the hip** and idiopathic **scoliosis** are other conditions PTs see.',
match('Pediatric conditions',[['Cerebral palsy','Non-progressive brain injury affecting movement'],['Spina bifida','Neural tube defect; latex allergy risk'],['Down syndrome','Low tone and ligament laxity'],['Duchenne muscular dystrophy','X-linked; Gowers’ sign'],['Torticollis','Tight sternocleidomastoid tilts the head'],['Scoliosis','Sideways curve of the spine']])]],
['Pediatric PT services and IDEA',[
'Under the **Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA)**, **Part C** provides **early intervention** for infants and toddlers from **birth to age 3**, delivered in natural environments such as the home and guided by an **Individualized Family Service Plan (IFSP)**. **Part B** provides special education and related services, including school-based PT, for children **ages 3–21**, guided by an **Individualized Education Program (IEP)**. School-based PTs help students move safely around campus, participate in physical education and use equipment such as walkers, wheelchairs and adapted seating.',
'Pediatric PT is **play-based** and **family-centered**: therapists use toys, games and daily routines, teach parents home activities and set goals that matter to the family. Equipment can include **orthoses** (such as ankle-foot orthoses), **gait trainers**, **standers**, adaptive strollers and power mobility, which even young children can learn to use.',
sort('IDEA Part C or Part B?',['Part C (birth to 3)','Part B (ages 3–21)'],[['Individualized Family Service Plan',0],['Individualized Education Program',1],['Therapy in the family’s home for an 18-month-old',0],['School-based PT for a 10-year-old',1],['Early intervention services',0],['PT so a student can access the classroom',1]])]],
['Normal aging',[
'With age, muscle mass and strength decline (**sarcopenia**), bones lose density, joints stiffen, reaction time slows, balance systems change and maximum heart rate and lung elasticity decrease. Skin becomes thinner and heals more slowly, and vision and hearing may decline. These changes are **not** the same as disease or frailty: **regular exercise**, including strength, balance and aerobic training, slows or reverses many of them at any age.',
'Many older adults have several chronic conditions and take multiple medications (**polypharmacy**), which can cause dizziness and falls. **Dementia** affects memory and judgment, so PTs simplify instructions, use routines and involve caregivers. **Medicare** covers much outpatient and home-health PT for people 65 and older when it is medically necessary.',
sort('Typical aging change or not?',['Common age-related change','Not a normal part of aging'],[['Gradual loss of muscle mass',0],['Slower reaction time',0],['Sudden confusion',1],['Thinner skin',0],['Inability to walk at all',1],['Lower maximum heart rate',0]])]],
['Falls in older adults',[
'About **one in four** adults aged 65 and older falls each year, and falls are the leading cause of injury and injury death in this age group. Risk factors include **muscle weakness**, **balance and gait problems**, **medications** (such as sedatives and blood pressure drugs), **vision problems**, foot problems, **orthostatic hypotension** and home hazards such as loose rugs and poor lighting. The CDC **STEADI** program asks three key questions: Have you fallen in the past year? Do you feel unsteady when standing or walking? Do you worry about falling?',
'PTs assess fall risk with tests such as the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)**—an older adult who takes **12 seconds or longer** to stand, walk 10 feet, turn, return and sit is at higher risk—the **30-second chair stand** and the **4-stage balance test**, along with the **Berg Balance Scale**. Interventions include **strength and balance exercise** (such as tai chi and progressive balance training), gait training and assistive devices, **home safety** changes (grab bars, lighting, removing rugs), footwear advice, medication review referrals and teaching how to **get up from the floor** safely.',
order('Timed Up and Go',['Patient sits in a chair with back against it','On “go,” the patient stands','Walks 10 feet at a normal pace','Turns around','Walks back','Sits down; stop the timer']),
kase('Fall risk visit',{a:{text:'An 80-year-old took 15 seconds on the Timed Up and Go and says she worries about falling. What does this suggest?',choices:[['Increased fall risk—she needs a full fall-risk assessment and intervention','b','Correct. A TUG of 12 seconds or longer suggests higher risk.',true],['Normal for her age; no action needed','b','Her time and fear of falling both signal risk.',false]]},b:{text:'Which intervention has strong evidence for reducing falls?',choices:[['Progressive strength and balance exercise such as tai chi','c','Correct. Exercise reduces falls in older adults.',true],['Bed rest to avoid walking','c','Inactivity increases weakness and falls.',false]]},c:{text:'The PT also recommends grab bars, better lighting and a medication review with her physician.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Milestones run head to toe: head control ~4 months, sitting ~6–9, walking ~12 (by 18 is a red flag limit); persistent primitive reflexes and lost skills are red flags.','CP (non-progressive, spastic most common), spina bifida (latex allergy, shunt), Down syndrome (hypotonia), Duchenne (X-linked, Gowers’ sign), torticollis; IDEA Part C (birth–3, IFSP) and Part B (3–21, IEP).','Aging brings sarcopenia and slower balance responses, but exercise helps; one in four older adults falls yearly; TUG ≥12 seconds suggests higher risk; strength, balance, home safety and medication review prevent falls.'],terms:[['Cerebral palsy','Non-progressive movement disorders from early brain injury.'],['Early intervention','IDEA Part C services for children from birth to age 3.'],['Sarcopenia','Age-related loss of muscle mass and strength.']],mcqs:[q('About when do most infants walk independently?','12 months','4 months','24 months','6 months','Range about 9–18 months.'),q('Cerebral palsy is best described as:','A non-progressive disorder from early brain injury','A progressive muscle disease','A spinal infection','A heart defect','Non-progressive.'),q('Which condition carries a high risk of latex allergy?','Spina bifida','Down syndrome','Torticollis','Scoliosis','Repeated early exposure.'),q('Gowers’ sign is associated with:','Duchenne muscular dystrophy','Cerebral palsy','Torticollis','Osteoarthritis','Pushing on the thighs to stand.'),q('IDEA Part C serves children:','From birth to age 3','From ages 3–21','Only in high school','Over age 65','Early intervention.'),q('What fraction of older adults fall each year?','About one in four','About one in 100','About three in four','Nearly all','CDC estimate.'),q('A Timed Up and Go of 12 seconds or longer suggests:','Higher fall risk','Excellent balance','Normal walking speed for teenagers','A heart problem','STEADI threshold.'),q('Age-related loss of muscle mass is called:','Sarcopenia','Spasticity','Contracture','Hypotonia','Slowed by strength training.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare how a physical therapist would approach care for a 2-year-old with spastic diplegic cerebral palsy and an 80-year-old who has fallen twice in a year, including assessment, intervention and the systems or laws that shape each service.',rubric:[['Child: condition and assessment',['cerebral palsy','non-progressive','spastic','diplegia','milestones','tone']],['Child: intervention and law',['play','family','orthosis','gait trainer','idea','part c','ifsp','early intervention']],['Older adult: assessment',['steadi','timed up and go','12 seconds','balance','strength','medications','orthostatic']],['Older adult: intervention',['strength','balance','tai chi','home safety','grab bars','assistive device','medicare']]],model:'Spastic diplegic cerebral palsy is a non-progressive movement disorder caused by injury to the developing brain, mainly affecting the legs with increased tone. For the 2-year-old, the PT assesses motor milestones, muscle tone, range of motion and how the child moves during play. Treatment is play-based and family-centered: stretching and positioning to limit contractures, strengthening through games, practice of sit-to-stand and walking, and equipment such as ankle-foot orthoses or a gait trainer. Under IDEA Part C, these early intervention services take place in natural environments such as the home and follow an Individualized Family Service Plan, and at age 3 the child moves to Part B services with an IEP. For the 80-year-old, the PT uses the CDC STEADI approach, asking about falls, unsteadiness and fear of falling. The assessment includes the Timed Up and Go—12 seconds or longer suggests higher risk—the 30-second chair stand and balance tests, plus checks of strength, orthostatic blood pressure, vision and medications. Interventions include progressive strength and balance training such as tai chi, gait training with an appropriate assistive device, home safety changes such as grab bars, lighting and removing rugs, and a referral for medication review. Medicare often covers this care when it is medically necessary.'},sources:[RULES,CDC_MILESTONES,CDC_CP,IDEA,CDC_STEADI]};
